From 6a6b1d62981fe33f5667abb7ec8c32e685f02eb0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FlominatorGD <75490337+FlominatorGD@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 01:37:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Disable ShadowCallStack for Exynos 7870 builds

The vendor GL driver blob shipped for the Exynos 7870 devices
(libGLES_mali.so, built for the stock 8.0 firmware with clang 6.0.2) uses
x18 as a general-purpose register: its .text contains 872 references to
x18.  bionic stores the ShadowCallStack pointer in x18
(__init_shadow_call_stack() is the only writer), so the blob destroys the
shadow stack pointer of every process it runs in -- and it does run
in-process, the graphics driver is preloaded by zygote unless
ro.zygote.disable_gl_preload is set, which this device does not.

The next SCS prologue then stores to [x18, ...] with x18 == 0 and the
process dies with a SIGSEGV/SEGV_MAPERR at address 0x0.  This is what the
boot log shows for the only two SCS-built modules of the build
(packages/modules/Bluetooth android/app and packages/apps/Nfc nci/jni):

  F libc: Fatal signal 11 (SIGSEGV), code 1 (SEGV_MAPERR), fault addr 0x0
  #00 pc .../libbluetooth_jni.so (__system_property_get+0)
  #01 pc .../libbluetooth_jni.so (__init_cpu_features+44)
  ...
  #00 pc .../libnfc_nci_jni.so (_GLOBAL__sub_I_HciEventManager.cpp+0)

Both modules are loaded by their app process (com.android.bluetooth,
com.android.nfc) right at start, so the crash loops kill Bluetooth and
NFC on every boot.

AOSP only recommends SCS for self-contained components that never run
next to legacy code (source.android.com/docs/security/test/shadow-call-stack),
which is not the case on these devices, so disable SCS for the whole
build instead of chasing individual modules.  SCS can be turned back on
if the vendor GL driver is ever replaced.
---
 cc/sanitize.go | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/cc/sanitize.go b/cc/sanitize.go
index d8d8c7a..9284ed9 100644
--- a/cc/sanitize.go
+++ b/cc/sanitize.go
@@ -645,6 +645,30 @@ func (sanitize *sanitize) begin(ctx BaseModuleContext) {
 		s.Scs = nil
 	}
 
+	// The vendor GL driver blob shipped for the Exynos 7870 devices
+	// (libGLES_mali.so, built for the stock 8.0 firmware with clang 6.0.2)
+	// uses x18 as a general-purpose register: its .text contains 872
+	// references to x18.  bionic stores the ShadowCallStack pointer in x18
+	// (__init_shadow_call_stack() is the only writer), so the blob destroys
+	// the shadow stack pointer of every process it runs in -- and it does run
+	// in-process, the graphics driver is preloaded by zygote unless
+	// ro.zygote.disable_gl_preload is set, which this device does not.
+	//
+	// The next SCS prologue then stores to [x18, ...] with x18 == 0 and the
+	// process dies with a SIGSEGV/SEGV_MAPERR at address 0x0.  This is what
+	// the boot log shows for the only two SCS-built modules of the build
+	// (packages/modules/Bluetooth android/app and packages/apps/Nfc nci/jni):
+	//
+	//   #00 pc .../libbluetooth_jni.so (__system_property_get+0)
+	//   #01 pc .../libbluetooth_jni.so (__init_cpu_features+44)
+	//   #00 pc .../libnfc_nci_jni.so (_GLOBAL__sub_I_HciEventManager.cpp+0)
+	//
+	// AOSP only recommends SCS for self-contained components that never run
+	// next to legacy code (source.android.com/docs/security/test/shadow-call-stack),
+	// which is not the case on these devices, so disable SCS for the whole
+	// build instead of chasing individual modules.
+	s.Scs = nil
+
 	// Memtag_heap is only implemented on AArch64.
 	// Memtag ABI is Android specific for now, so disable for host.
 	if ctx.Arch().ArchType != android.Arm64 || !ctx.toolchain().Bionic() || ctx.Host() {
